<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint-Quentin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augusta Viromanduorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint-Quentin is a town on the Somme in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. As Augusta Viromanduorum it was the Roman chief town of the Viromandui, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of the counts of Vermandois, growing around the shrine of the martyr Quentin. A Spanish army defeated the French there in 1557, and the town was heavily damaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p></div>
